DESCRIPTION:Situated in Black media studies and play studies\, Zayla Crock
 er examines Bernard Rose’s 1992 film Candyman along with Nia DaCosta’s
  remake of the same name to demonstrate how both films encourage the spect
 ator to “play” within the genre conventions of the slasher film throug
 h an act of ritualized play Crocker deems “playing wretched.” Both fil
 ms overtly or covertly prompt the spectator to call on and summon the perf
 ormance of Black memory\, history\, and trauma in the figure of a hook-han
 ded slash\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-11-09 14:54 PST
